  "structure": {
    "block_count": 22,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "When Toddlers Touch Themselves: Why It Happens and How to Respond",
        "text_sha256": "94ef136493fdb78ad5311b7683c797edb162c447f55100704e5187b5e4f64029",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Light copy updates, updates to sources, and fact-checked for accuracy.",
        "text_sha256": "03ab6ec71ffcc7c2416587f4ad58dc4805f0a11bd13223d387ea9b28fc87f850",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "toddler on a couch",
        "text_sha256": "1d8ad237923f5524fe23587def254f79ca8e909e1d62b372d200664ccb2d083b",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Toddlers are naturally curious about exploring their whole bodies, including their genitals. Here's how to handle this normal stage of development.",
        "text_sha256": "effae00abed1ccd5e364621bec910dcb1e07c70293e6c83ddaaaa45a3378f80a",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "When your little one starts touching herself, don't worry, stress, or get embarrassed. Your toddler has discovered a part of her body that may have gone largely unexplored during infancy. What's more, she might have learned that touching her genitals feels good.",
        "text_sha256": "8ca2714718a57f9cb742adf6f9d95bcf8e9cfd2401a844e95efed051543ad76f",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Key Takeaways",
        "text_sha256": "cc3f7f0f4513969e418b56fb93ec0876fd96dd6498395b04301f70dbddff8a45",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Genital touching in toddlers is normal and age-appropriate, often driven by curiosity and new bodily awareness (especially during potty training).",
        "text_sha256": "e47ed32e0e67bed7e78d738f92c26c21781eff8f607425ac10dd0ccbceec9bde",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Parents should avoid shaming or scolding, instead ignoring the behavior at home and gently redirecting or distracting in public; begin teaching the idea of “private vs. public” as children mature.",
        "text_sha256": "708205f4bd26675a300111e9a75f4b7723a9b7a4356b2a43616335ab9887ed06",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Check for underlying needs or concerns, such as needing to use the bathroom or possible stress, and consult a pediatrician if the behavior becomes constant or obsessive.",
        "text_sha256": "aeca5e1ba8f838a021face21eea391f5acd0d4e25f8e924eb71864a78f58aeb7",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Why do toddlers touch themselves?",
        "text_sha256": "fc1f316ca5873203cea24f8974234473e59a3707a9927c37f7142bed32aa8333",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your child's new discovery of her genitals is fueled by age-appropriate curiosity and is as innocent as her exploration of her fingers and toes. You may notice this behavior around the time you switch her to training pants (accessibility is key) or when you begin potty-training (her private parts have now become the focus of a lot of public attention). Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsSexual Behaviors in Young Children: What%u2019s Normal, What%u2019s Not?See All Sources [1] Why does she keep doing it? Simple — because it feels good.",
        "text_sha256": "6baa0dad40b89acb259c37205b864388ea065e14fa2c6f8e4d3ad05575c93d0d",
        "word_count": 88
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceAmerican Academy of PediatricsSexual Behaviors in Young Children: What%u2019s Normal, What%u2019s Not?See All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "a6f1823fc8a4a3eea39e57ab622ef865e35596af6ff0fb2e08e3f509584cea26",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What you need to know",
        "text_sha256": "9096ab0e1fa3cc166831998c357e800c4ff2d1bcae7bd36db142c5f1b0e49400",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your toddler's touching of her genitals isn't masturbation (and that also goes for little boys who get erections while playing with their penises). Yes, it feels good, and even comforting, but there's nothing sexual about a young child's intent or emotions.",
        "text_sha256": "60432192c929b98227fbae9cefbd9e3d674f185b4ff57cb77d36f2b728f322f0",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What to do about it",
        "text_sha256": "091a449b6d9e6247c6f7cb75ceb75ddda3d52abe27d14e5a1d94373b2c3fa605",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "How you should react depends on where you are.",
        "text_sha256": "1039e82c4be73bccfae60656b5a368c4da802295888650c0e244ce2bb1729f14",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Don't forbid, shame, scold, or rebuke your child. Not only might your negative response make the behavior more tempting, it'll also send the message that she should be ashamed of her body and feelings associated with it. So try not to make any fuss. In fact, if you're at home, ignore the behavior.",
        "text_sha256": "43cbe7faab27f18a4fa1fc45547b9a2149948ed1f519646de28b484e85a0f93c",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "In semi-public settings, such as playgroups or extended family gatherings, distract your child with another hands-on activity, such as drawing with crayons or building with blocks. If she can't be persuaded to change course, abandon ship and look away. Other toddlers won't care and your friends and family will understand.",
        "text_sha256": "78b36b38e82a2bb049ca0db091d51aaa4bdb762d8f6eee208d4f16205a1e4fbd",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Begin teaching your child the difference between \"public\" and \"private.\" If she starts touching herself while you're out in public, quietly tell her that some things are okay to do in private but not in public where there are people around. Take her hand, give it a gentle squeeze, and distract her. Don't forget to also praise her for being able to wait until she's home.",
        "text_sha256": "04a0260f2e9235dede21ea1473a52d96c9ad6f3601128215b30a1d930f5fb0d7",
        "word_count": 66
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Ask if she needs to go to the bathroom. Some toddlers hold their genitals when they have to pee. If you suspect yours is one of them, make a habit of asking.",
        "text_sha256": "512e15b834ac4e12ce640afd07390f0507a25187875e5d5d832bbefc29128941",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your toddler seems to become obsessed with or consumed by her \"touchy\" habit, talk to your pediatrician. Sometimes underlying stress is the culprit.",
        "text_sha256": "5b914ff7abf0b5de53d74b45544f7bae5d98be90e05ff6c8d07f9e199faf5251",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Have more questions about toddler behavior? Open the What to Expect app for your complete guide to the toddler years, plus tips from other parents who have been there.",
        "text_sha256": "d777746f691b8ecffad2c19b0870e524aa4c808a8eaf638535e60786af6dfb19",
        "word_count": 29
      }
    ],
    "content_word_count": 589,
    "heading_count": 5
  },
